But note: Go arrays are value types, but we can use them. However, 55*55*205 = 618,000 elements, each int64 (8 bytes) ~ 5MB, fine.

We need to read input: n, lim. Then read n integers. Count A (50) and B (100). Then initialize dp[A][B][0] = 1.

Compute combinations: c[0][0] = 1. For i from 1 to 50: for j from 0 to i: c[i][j] = c[i-1][j-1] + c[i-1][j] (with bounds check: if j-1 < 0 then 0). In C, c[i-1][j-1] when j=0 accesses c[i-1][-1] which is 0 because global array zero-initialized. In Go, we need to handle boundaries: if j > 0 then add c[i-1][j-1]. So we can do:
